Right then.

Last chapter, we had a bath.

And what a bloody bath it was.

We started with some water, moved on to fire, wind, earth, the Moon and Sun, eventually found ourselves in a cremation ground, and somehow ended up discovering that the only real purification is immersion in Bhairava.

Absolutely magnificent.

Now, we’ve established something rather important.

Your true nature is Śiva.

Your apparent impurity arises through the conviction that you’re something other than Śiva.

And ritual purification is designed to overcome that conviction.

Lovely.

But we’ve still got a problem.

How exactly do you perform worship without reinforcing the very duality you’re trying to overcome?

Think about it.

You’re a little individual.

There’s a deity over there.

You bring the deity some flowers.

You chant a few mantras.

And presumably you hope the deity will be pleased.

But hang on.

Haven’t I spent twelve chapters telling you there’s nothing outside Śiva?

Who exactly is offering what to whom?

If Śiva is everything, isn’t the worshipper Śiva?

Aren’t the flowers Śiva?

Isn’t the altar Śiva?

And isn’t the very act of worship also Śiva?

Precisely.

And that’s the entire point of this chapter.

We’re going to turn ritual into a direct enactment of nonduality.

Not merely thinking that everything is Śiva.

But arranging every component of worship so that nothing remains conceptually outside him.

And then we’re going to use this very ritual structure to introduce a disciple into the tradition.

Samaya-dīkṣā.

Initiation into the observances.

