Right then.

Last chapter was quite an undertaking, wasn’t it?

We started by choosing somewhere pleasant to worship.

Then we installed the entire divine alphabet into the body.

Built a trident inside ourselves.

Established Śiva and Śakti in the ritual fire.

Consecrated the pots.

Worshipped the directional guardians.

Blindfolded a disciple.

Removed the blindfold.

Burned their bonds with the fiery right hand.

Nourished their pure nature with the lunar left.

And performed forty-eight consecrations.

Absolutely magnificent.

And what did the disciple become?

Samayī.

Someone formally initiated into the observances of the tradition.

Now that’s lovely.

They can practise their mantra.

Perform daily worship.

Study the scriptures.

Attend upon their guru.

But here’s the thing.

We haven’t yet performed the initiation that liberates the disciple from the full structure of their bonds.

That comes now.

Putrakadīkṣā.

Let’s break down the name.

Putraka — literally a little son, here the spiritual son of the guru.

Dīkṣā — initiation.

So this is the initiation through which the disciple enters into a deeper relationship with the tradition and its liberating powers.

Now, you’re probably thinking:

“Abhinava, mate, how complicated is this going to be?”

Well.

You know those six cosmic pathways we spent several chapters studying?

All thirty-six tattvas?

The five kalās?

The worlds?

The mantras?

The phonemes?

We’re going to need the whole bloody lot.

And then we’re going to dissolve the entire thing.

Inside the disciple.

Lovely.

1. First, Abhinavagupta admits he’s giving you the short version
The chapter begins:

अथ पुत्रकदीक्षाविधिः।

Atha putrakadīkṣāvidhiḥ.

Now, the procedure for putraka initiation.

And immediately:

Sa ca vistīrṇas tantrālokād avadhāryaḥ.

The extensive procedure should be understood from the Tantrāloka.

Then:

Saṅkṣiptas tūcyate.

But the condensed version is explained here.

Of course it is.

We’ve already had thirteen chapters of metaphysics and ritual.

And apparently this is still the abbreviated handbook.

If you want the full thing, read my enormous Tantrāloka.

Right.

Fair enough.

Now, the first thing to understand is that this initiation doesn’t begin from nowhere.

The preliminary rites have already been performed.

Remember the samayī initiation?

The worship.

The fire.

The observances.

The consecrations.

All of that serves as the preparation.

And then, on the third day, the guru performs the next major ceremony.

Where?

Within the:

Triśūlābja-maṇḍala.

The maṇḍala of the trident and lotus.

Remember the cosmic trident from Chapter Thirteen?

Well, it’s back.

Only now it becomes the principal arrangement for an initiation that involves the entire cosmic hierarchy.

2. The third day — welcome to the cosmic initiation chamber
Right.

Imagine the ritual ground.

The maṇḍala has been established.

The trident-and-lotus structure is arranged.

And we’re going to worship the entire collective of divine powers.

Sāmudāyika-yāga.

Collective worship.

But, as before, we don’t immediately charge into the central maṇḍala.

First:

Bāhya-parivāra.

The outer divine retinue.

Then:

Dvāra-devatā-cakra.

The circle of doorway deities.

Because apparently the doorway continues to require its own divine security clearance.

“Excuse me, who’s there?”

“Śiva.”

“Which Śiva?”

“The supreme Lord.”

“Very good. Carry on.”

Now, the guru proceeds to the eastern part of the maṇḍala.

Beginning at:

Aiśa-koṇa.

The northeast corner.

And moving toward:

Āgneya.

The southeast.

And along this sequence, the guru worships eight divine or authoritative recipients.

First:

Gaṇapati.

The lord of the hosts, remover of obstacles.

Second:

Guru.

The immediate teacher.

Third:

Paramaguru.

The teacher’s teacher.

Fourth:

Parameṣṭhin.

The more remote or supreme predecessor in the lineage.

Fifth:

Pūrvācāryāḥ.

The earlier teachers.

Sixth:

Yoginī-cakra.

The circle of Yoginīs.

Seventh:

Vāgīśvarī.

The goddess of speech.

Eighth:

Kṣetrapāla.

The guardian of the sacred ground.

Now, isn’t that a remarkable arrangement?

You’ve got the remover of obstacles.

The whole teaching lineage.

The divine feminine powers.

The goddess governing speech.

And the guardian of the place.

The initiation is situated within a complete ritual and transmission structure.

Not merely one individual performing something upon another.

The guru is invoking the authorities and divine powers through which the teaching is understood to operate.

And that’s important.

Because the initiation we’re about to perform is not just a private psychological exercise.

It’s a formal tantric ritual.

The disciple is entering a consecrated relationship with the divine hierarchy.

Now.

We’ve worshipped the perimeter.

The lineage.

The Yoginīs.

The guardian.

And Abhinavagupta says the guru must obtain the appropriate permission.

Ājñā.

Authorisation or command.

Then the really interesting work begins.

We’re going to install the entire universe into the maṇḍala.

3. Install the six cosmic pathways in the trident
Right.

Remember Chapter Ten?

We established the six adhvans.

The six pathways through which manifestation is articulated.

Three objective.

Three expressive.

THE SIX PATHWAYS BEING CONSECRATED

Bhuvana

Worlds and realms

Tattva

Principles of reality

Kalā

Great cosmic divisions

Pada

Articulated expressions

Mantra

Sacred phonemic formulae

Varṇa

Letters and sounds

Now imagine the guru contemplating the entirety of these pathways.

Not merely one little deity.

Not merely the physical altar.

The whole structure of manifestation.

And the Sanskrit says:

Śūlamūlāt prabhṛti sitakamalāntam.

From the root of the trident up to the white lotus.

The entire path is installed and worshipped.

Samastam adhvānaṃ nyasyārcayet.

Now, that’s magnificent.

Because the trident-and-lotus maṇḍala becomes a map of the entire manifested universe.

Every level.

Every principle.

Every sacred sound.

Everything.

And through nyāsa, the cosmic structure is ritually established within the maṇḍala.

But now we need the presiding deities.

And here’s where the three great Trika goddesses enter.

Parā.

Parāparā.

Aparā.

Now pay attention.

Because Abhinavagupta gives their actual placements.

And they’re rather precise.

4. The three goddesses take their places
First, the middle trident.

Mahya-triśūla — the central trident.

And upon its middle prong:

Śrī Parā Bhaṭṭārikā.

The venerable supreme goddess Parā.

But she’s not alone.

She’s there with:

Bhairavanātha.

The Lord Bhairava.

Now, that’s the central divine pair.

Consciousness and its supreme power.

And on the left prong of that central trident?

Śrīmad Aparā.

The venerable Aparā.

On the right?

Śrī Parāparā.

Now, before you confuse the sides, here’s the arrangement.

CENTRAL TRIDENT — SCHEMATIC PLACEMENT

Aparā

Left

Parā + Bhairava

Middle

Parāparā

Right

Orientation as described by the Sanskrit’s left, middle and right prongs.

Now here’s the subtle detail.

The central trident isn’t the only one.

On the trident to the right, Parāparā occupies the middle.

On the trident to the left, Aparā occupies the middle.

And their corresponding pairs of subsidiary forms occupy the other positions, according to the ritual arrangement.

So we’ve got a threefold configuration.

Parā presiding centrally.

Parāparā in her appropriate right-hand domain.

Aparā in her left-hand domain.

And each participates in the complete divine manifestation.

Now, don’t imagine these are merely three separate goddesses renting different sections of the altar.

Remember what we established in Chapter Four.

The triad represents the powers of unity, unity-in-difference, and differentiation.

Abheda.

Bhedābheda.

Bheda.

But all three belong to one consciousness.

And Abhinavagupta now gives the reason the entire structure works.

5. The goddess fills every place
Here’s the Sanskrit principle:

Sarvasthānādhiṣṭhātṛtva.

The condition of presiding over every place.

And Abhinavagupta says that because the goddess presides over all the locations, everything becomes full through her presence.

Sarvaṃ pūrṇam.

Now that’s profound.

Because you might look at the ritual arrangement and think:

“Well, Parā is in this position, and Aparā is in that position.”

But ultimately the divine power isn’t confined to the little spot on the maṇḍala where she’s being worshipped.

Her manifestation pervades the arrangement.

The ritual establishes differentiated positions.

But its purpose is to reveal one divine presence.

And now Abhinavagupta gives a beautiful instruction.

On the middle prong of the central trident, worship the entire circle of deities as non-different.

Abhinnatayā.

Without division.

From the central divine powers right down to:

Lokapāla.

The guardians of the directions.

And:

Astra.

The protective weapon-powers.

All of them.

Now imagine someone asking:

“Abhinava, haven’t we already worshipped some of these elsewhere?”

Yes.

“But why do we need to worship the entire circle again in the centre?”

Because the central divine power pervades the whole arrangement.

Worshipping the entire circle in non-difference with her means acknowledging that nothing in the system stands outside her sovereignty.

The many are ritually gathered into the one.

And now he gives us an instruction that I absolutely love.

Because he’s about to unite five things that, at first glance, look completely separate.

The two sacred vessels.

The maṇḍala.

The fire.

And the worshipper.

6. The five seats — the pots, the altar, the fire, and YOU
Now, here’s the instruction.

Pañcādhikaraṇam anusandhiṃ kuryāt.

Establish the conjunction of five supports.

And what are they?

1

Kumbha

The first sacred vessel

2

Kalaśa

The second sacred vessel

3

Maṇḍala

The divine diagram

4

Agni

The ritual fire

5

Svātman

One’s own Self

Now, understand what’s happening.

You’ve got the kumbha.

One vessel.

The kalaśa.

Another vessel.

The maṇḍala, containing the entire divine arrangement.

The fire, through which offerings are made.

And the worshipper’s own Self.

