Not by throwing yourself into the maṇḍala.

Please don’t.

But through the recognition that their essential reality isn’t separate from Parameśvara.

And now the special worship begins.

Viśeṣa-pūjā.

Using flowers and other offerings that have been filled with the flavour of nonduality.

And the Sanskrit gives us a magnificent compound:

Parameśvarādvayarasabṛṃhita.

Parameśvara — the supreme Lord.

Advaya — nondual.

Rasa — flavour, essence, relish.

Bṛṃhita — expanded, nourished, made full.

Filled and enriched with the flavour of non-difference from the supreme Lord.

Now that’s a beautiful description of ritual.

The flowers aren’t merely flowers.

The very offering is saturated with the understanding of nonduality.

And the same goes for the worshipper.

You don’t approach the divine as an independently existing stranger hoping to win its favour.

You’re approaching the ritual through the recognition that the whole field is an expression of divine consciousness.

Now, Abhinavagupta makes a very practical point.

And it’s not one you’ll find on every inspirational yoga poster.

7. Don’t be stingy — but don’t perform the expensive ritual if you’re skint
Right.

The Sanskrit says:

Kiṃ bahunā.

Why say more?

In other words:

“Right, enough explaining. Here’s the practical point.”

The ritual place should be filled with:

Tarpaṇa.

Satisfying offerings.

And:

Naivedya.

Food offerings.

The guru shouldn’t be deceitful or miserly concerning the resources available for the ceremony.

Vittaśāṭhya-virahita.

Without financial dishonesty or stinginess.

Now, that might sound rather obvious.

But remember that this is a major public ritual involving multiple divine powers, consecrated vessels, offerings, fire and a maṇḍala.

It’s not just sitting in the corner and quietly contemplating a Sanskrit syllable.

Resources are required.

And here’s the particularly striking instruction:

Asati vitte tu mahāmaṇḍalayāgo na kartavya eva.

If sufficient wealth is absent, the great maṇḍala worship simply should not be undertaken.

Now, I rather appreciate that.

It’s not:

“Borrow money you haven’t got.”

“Sell all your possessions.”

“Take out an enormous loan to pay for the spiritual package.”

No.

If the resources aren’t there, don’t perform this particular elaborate rite.

Remember the earlier chapters.

There are other ways of practising and recognising Śiva.

This is one specific ritual with particular requirements.

And Abhinavagupta isn’t pretending it can be carried out properly without them.

But now comes a much more difficult passage.

And we’re not going to clean it up just because it makes modern readers uncomfortable.

8. The animal offerings — the uncomfortable part of the original Tantra
Right.

Abhinavagupta instructs that living animals be presented in the rite.

Paśūṃś ca jīvato nivedayet.

One should present animals while they are alive.

And he gives a theological explanation.

He says that the animals themselves receive:

